    "publishingNote": null,
    "markdown": "Thanksgiving break. Winter break. A flight to a conference or to see family. For a lot of Sikh students, airport security is the part of the trip they dread most. Will they ask me to take off my turban? What do I do with my kirpan? What if the officer does not know what either one is?\n\nHere is what TSA's own pages say, plus practical tips so you walk up to the checkpoint prepared and calm.\n\nThis post is general information, not legal advice. Rules can change, so check TSA's site before you fly.\n\n## The short answer\n\nTSA does not allow kirpans in carry-on bags. They must be packed in checked baggage, sheathed or securely wrapped. You may keep a turban or other religious head covering on during screening, but it may trigger additional screening, such as a pat-down by an officer of the same sex. If an alarm cannot be resolved, you may ask to remove the head covering in a private screening area.\n\n## Kirpans: carry-on vs. checked\n\nTSA's \"What Can I Bring?\" page for kirpans says:\n\n- **Carry-on bags: No**\n- **Checked bags: Yes**\n- Sharp objects in checked bags should be **sheathed or securely wrapped** to protect baggage handlers and inspectors.\n- The **final decision rests with the TSA officer** at the checkpoint.\n\nTSA's FAQ adds that religious knives, swords, and other objects are not permitted through the security checkpoint and must be packed in checked baggage.\n\nFor Sikhs who wear a kirpan as an article of faith, this is a real conflict, and many Sikhs and Sikh organizations have raised concerns about it. How you handle it is a personal decision. [NEEDS GURSIMAR REVIEW] Some practical notes:\n\n- **Plan before you pack.** If you are flying carry-on only, think through your plan ahead of time.\n- **Pack it carefully.** Use a sheath and wrap it securely in checked luggage.\n- **International flights** have their own rules depending on the country and airline. Check before you go.\n\n## Turbans and head coverings at screening\n\nFrom TSA's FAQ and religion pages:\n\n- You **may keep your head covering on** during screening.\n- Head coverings and loose or bulky clothing **may get additional screening**, which can include a pat-down.\n- Pat-downs are done by **an officer of the same sex**.\n- If an alarm cannot be resolved with a pat-down, **you may ask to remove the head covering in a private screening area**.\n- At any time, you can **request private screening** and have a **witness of your choice** present.\n- If you have concerns during screening, you can **ask to speak with a supervisor**.\n\nTSA also notes officers are trained to screen religious items with respect and care, and that you should tell an officer if you have religious items that need special handling.\n\n## What screening may look like\n\nStudents describe a few common scenarios:\n\n1. **You walk through and nothing happens.** It does happen.\n2. **The officer asks to pat down your turban.** You can ask them to explain what they will do first.\n3. **You may be asked to touch your own turban** and have your hands swabbed. Some travelers report this as an option; ask the officer. [VERIFY current TSA practice]\n4. **An alarm is not resolved.** You can ask for private screening before anything is removed.\n\n## Tips from the road\n\n- **Arrive early.** Extra screening takes time.\n- **Know your words.** \"This is a religious head covering. I would like to keep it on. If you need to screen it, please explain the process, and I would like private screening if anything needs to be removed.\"\n- **Stay calm and polite**, even if the officer is not familiar with Sikhs.\n- **Ask for a supervisor** if something feels wrong.\n- **Write it down afterward:** airport, time, officer names if available. TSA accepts feedback through its Contact Center.\n- **Consider TSA PreCheck** if you fly often; it is a paid program, so weigh the cost.\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\nIf you feel you were treated unfairly at screening, TSA says you can speak with a supervisor at the checkpoint and submit your concern to the TSA Contact Center. Sikh civil rights organizations such as the Sikh Coalition and SALDEF also offer know-your-rights resources for travelers. [VERIFY current resource links]\n\nOn campus, USM's advocacy program works so chapters have backing instead of having to fight issues alone. If an incident affects your SSA or campus, reach out.\n\n## Traveling to a USM event?\n\nMany students fly to USM conferences and retreats. If your chapter is traveling together, share this post in your group chat before the trip.\n\n- **[See upcoming USM events](/events)**\n- **[Learn about USM advocacy](/programs/advocacy)**\n- **[Find your SSA](/ssas)**\n- **Join the USM newsletter** in the site footer\n",
