    "slug": "how-to-start-a-sikh-student-association",
    "sourceFile": "how-to-start-a-sikh-student-association.md",
    "title": "How to Start a Sikh Student Association on Campus",
    "seoTitle": "How to Start a Sikh Student Association on Campus",
    "excerpt": "Step-by-step guide to starting a Sikh Student Association at your college: finding members, registering, first events, and support from USM.",
    "keywords": [
      "how to start a sikh student association",
      "start a sikh club in college",
      "sikh student association",
      "SSA college",
      "sikh club ideas"
    ],
    "cluster": "Sikh student organizing (hub)",
    "category": "Student Life",
    "author": "USM",
    "draft": true,
    "publishingNote": null,
    "markdown": "You walk into your first week of college and notice there is a club for almost everything. Rock climbing. Mock trial. Three different anime groups. But when you search the student org directory for \"Sikh,\" nothing comes up.\n\nThat moment is where a lot of Sikh Student Associations (SSAs) begin. Not with a big plan, but with one student who decides that the next Sikh freshman on campus should not have to feel that quiet surprise.\n\nThis guide walks through how to actually do it, from finding your first three members to running an event people talk about.\n\n## The short answer\n\nTo start a Sikh Student Association, find two or three committed students, check your school's student organization registration rules, write a simple constitution, recruit a faculty or staff advisor, register the club, and host a low-pressure first event. Then connect with a national network like United Sikh Movement so you are not building everything from scratch.\n\n## Step 1: Find your first few people\n\nYou do not need a crowd. You need a core.\n\n- **Ask around in person.** Look for patkas, dastaars, karas, or just ask friends from your gurdwara back home who else is going to your school.\n- **Post in class and dorm group chats.** A simple message works: \"Anyone interested in starting a Sikh Student Association? Free chai at the first meeting.\"\n- **Check with the local gurdwara.** Many sangats know which families have kids at nearby colleges.\n- **Remember SSAs are not only for Sikhs.** Friends who are curious, supportive, or want to learn are welcome, and they often become your strongest volunteers.\n\nThree people who show up every week beats thirty who signed a sheet once.\n\n## Step 2: Learn your school's rules\n\nEvery campus does this differently. Look for the office of student life, student activities, or student government. Common requirements include:\n\n- A minimum number of members (often somewhere between three and ten, but check your school)\n- A faculty or staff advisor\n- A written constitution or bylaws\n- Officer names and contact info\n- A training session for new club leaders\n\nWrite down the deadlines. Many schools only accept new organizations during certain windows each term.\n\n## Step 3: Write a simple constitution\n\nYour constitution does not need to be long. Most schools give you a template. Cover:\n\n1. **Name** (for example, \"[School] Sikh Student Association\")\n2. **Purpose**, in your own words. Something like: building community for Sikh students, sharing Sikh culture and values with campus, and doing seva together.\n3. **Membership**, open to all students\n4. **Officers and how they are chosen**\n5. **Meetings and decision-making**\n6. **How to amend the constitution**\n\nKeep the purpose statement broad enough that future boards can grow into it.\n\n## Step 4: Recruit an advisor\n\nLook for a professor or staff member who is Sikh, has worked with cultural or faith groups, or simply cares about students. Send a short, respectful email explaining what you are starting and what you would need from them (usually signing forms and attending a meeting or two per term). Many advisors say yes because nobody has asked them before.\n\n## Step 5: Pick roles that match the work\n\nAt the start, keep it small:\n\n- **President:** keeps the vision and runs meetings\n- **Vice President:** backs up the president and handles campus relations\n- **Treasurer:** budget, funding requests, reimbursements\n- **Events/Outreach lead:** plans programming and social media\n\nAs you grow, you can add seva, education, and alumni roles.\n\n## Step 6: Plan a first event people actually want to attend\n\nYour first event sets the tone. Make it easy to say yes to:\n\n- **Chai and samosas meet-and-greet.** Food is the universal RSVP.\n- **\"Sikhi 101\" open night** for friends and classmates, led by members comfortable speaking about their own experience.\n- **Turban tying table** on a campus lawn, a well-known way SSAs share Sikh identity in a hands-on, friendly way.\n- **Langar-style community meal or seva drive**, done with care and, ideally, guidance from your local gurdwara. [NEEDS GURSIMAR REVIEW]\n\nTake photos (with permission), collect emails, and follow up within 48 hours.\n\n## Step 7: Find funding\n\nMost schools have student government funding for registered clubs. Learn the request process early, since deadlines often come weeks before an event. Other sources include local gurdwaras, Sikh businesses, alumni, and national organizations. United Sikh Movement lists funding among the resources it provides to chapters. [VERIFY specifics of chapter funding]\n\n## Step 8: Do not do it alone\n\nStarting an SSA is a lot. The students who do it best usually have backup: someone who has done it before, templates that work, and a bigger community to lean on.\n\nUnited Sikh Movement supports a network of 40 Sikh Student Associations across the country with leadership retreats, regional conferences, funding, and resources. Chapters have used that support for things like opening an interfaith reflection room at UCSB with the Muslim Student Association and passing a student government resolution recognizing 1984 at UCI.\n\n**Starting an SSA or running one that needs backup? [Apply to join the USM network](/chapters/apply) [VERIFY URL].**\n\n## Common mistakes to avoid\n\n- **Waiting for the perfect board.** Start with who you have.\n- **Only planning big events.** Small weekly hangouts build the community that big events need.\n- **Skipping the handoff.** Many clubs fade when founders graduate. Recruit first- and second-years into leadership early and keep a shared drive of passwords, templates, and contacts.\n- **Burning out.** Split the work. Seva includes taking care of your team.\n",
