    "title": "Langar Wali Dal for a Campus Crowd",
    "seoTitle": "Langar Wali Dal for a Campus Crowd: SSA Guide",
    "excerpt": "Planning a campus langar? How SSAs cook langar wali dal for a crowd: working with a gurdwara, scaling, food safety, volunteers, and serving everyone with seva.",
    "keywords": [
      "langar dal recipe",
      "langar wali dal recipe",
      "gurudwara langar recipes",
      "langar recipes",
      "langar food recipes"
    ],
    "cluster": "Langar Kitchen for Students",
    "category": "Student Life",
    "author": "USM",
    "draft": true,
    "publishingNote": null,
    "markdown": "There is a reason people get nostalgic about gurdwara dal. It is simple, warm, and somehow tastes better than anything you make at home. And if your Sikh Student Association is planning a langar-style dinner on campus, dal is probably the first thing on the menu.\n\nThis guide is about the hard part nobody tells you: cooking it for 100 or 200 classmates, in a campus setting, without chaos. It is a planning guide first and a recipe second, and the recipe section is meant to be filled in with your local gurdwara's version.\n\n## The short answer\n\nLangar wali dal is the lentil dish commonly served at gurdwara langar. To make it for a campus crowd, partner with a local gurdwara's kitchen sevadars for their recipe and quantities, follow your campus food safety rules, plan volunteers for cooking, serving, and cleanup, and keep the spirit of langar: free, vegetarian, and open to everyone. [NEEDS GURSIMAR REVIEW]\n\n## First, what makes it \"langar wali\"?\n\n\"Langar wali dal\" just means \"langar-style dal,\" the kind served in the free community kitchen at the gurdwara. Langar is vegetarian, cooked and served by volunteers, and open to anyone. Cooking it is seva, selfless service. [NEEDS GURSIMAR REVIEW] Our post \"What Is Langar?\" covers the background.\n\nMany families and gurdwaras make it with a mix of lentils simmered slowly, then finished with a tadka (spices tempered in ghee or oil). Recipes vary a lot by family and region. [VERIFY with gurdwara sevadars]\n\n## Step 1: Talk to a gurdwara\n\nBefore you buy a single bag of lentils, call your local gurdwara. Kitchen sevadars cook for hundreds of people every week. They can help with:\n\n- Their dal recipe, scaled for your headcount\n- Large pots and equipment you may be able to borrow\n- Volunteers who know the process\n- Guidance on doing langar respectfully [NEEDS GURSIMAR REVIEW]\n\nSome SSAs even cook at the gurdwara and transport the food to campus. Ask what is possible.\n\n## Step 2: Check campus food rules\n\nThis is where many student events get stuck. Most colleges have rules about serving food at events, especially food cooked off campus. Common requirements include:\n\n- Using an approved caterer or campus dining\n- A temporary food permit\n- A food handler on site\n- Rules about homemade food\n\nAsk your student activities office early, ideally six to eight weeks before the event. If home-cooked food is not allowed, ask whether a gurdwara kitchen counts, or partner with a local Punjabi restaurant that is an approved vendor.\n\n## Step 3: Estimate quantities\n\nYour gurdwara will know best, but here is how to think about it:\n\n- **RSVPs plus a buffer.** Free food events usually draw walk-ins.\n- **One main plus sides.** Dal, a sabzi, rice or roti, and something sweet like kheer is a common langar-style spread. [VERIFY]\n- **Keep it simple** for your first event. One great dal beats five rushed dishes.\n\n## Step 4: The recipe\n\n**Ingredients and method:** to be supplied by your partner gurdwara's sevadars or Gursimar. We are intentionally not publishing a recipe we have not tested and sourced. [VERIFY]\n\nPlaceholder structure for the final recipe:\n\n1. Lentils used and soaking time\n2. Cooking time and method for a large batch\n3. Tadka ingredients\n4. Quantities per 50 people\n5. Holding temperature and serving tips\n\n## Step 5: Volunteers and roles\n\n| Role | What they do |\n|---|---|\n| Kitchen lead | Coordinates with the gurdwara and runs cooking |\n| Food safety lead | Handles permits, gloves, temperatures |\n| Setup crew | Mats or tables, signage, serving station |\n| Servers | Walk the rows or staff the line |\n| Welcome table | Greets guests, explains langar, collects emails |\n| Cleanup crew | Leaves the space better than you found it |\n\nServers usually cover their heads and wear gloves. Brief volunteers on how to explain langar to first-time guests. [NEEDS GURSIMAR REVIEW]\n\n## Step 6: Serving the langar way\n\n- **Everyone eats the same food**, together.\n- **Seating on the floor in rows** is traditional. If your venue or guests need it, chairs are fine. [NEEDS GURSIMAR REVIEW]\n- **Free for all.** No tickets, no pricing tiers. A donation box is optional.\n- **No waste.** Encourage guests to take what they will eat.\n\n## Step 7: After the event\n\n- Thank the gurdwara and volunteers publicly.\n- Post photos (with permission).\n- Email everyone who signed in with your next event.\n- Write down what worked for next year's board.\n\n## Want help planning?\n\nUSM's starter kit for new chapters includes a first-event playbook and a funding guide. If you are running an established SSA, check the USM events calendar for ideas from other chapters.\n\n- **[Start an SSA](/start-an-ssa)**\n- **[See upcoming events](/events)**\n- **[Find your SSA](/ssas)**\n- **Join the USM newsletter** in the site footer\n",
